Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = illustrated children's picture-book; softly painted storybook worlds, expressive handcrafted toys, and naturalistic animals in one unified visual style.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A fat, bunchy VELVETEEN RABBIT with spotted brown-and-white velveteen, pink sateen ears, thread whiskers, and a holly sprig between his paws sits atop the BOY'S stocking.

(SOUND: crackling fire, paper rustling, distant happy voices)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

The BOY, a small child in a white nightshirt, lifts the Rabbit from the stocking and hugs him.

(SOUND: a toy engine whirs and a clockwork mouse clicks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him. Then Aunts and Uncles came to dinner, and in the excitement of new presents the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

The Rabbit lies alone amid torn tissue paper as the Boy runs to brighter new toys.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Moonlight falls across a toy cupboard and a cold nursery fender. The Rabbit sits among a MODEL BOAT, a CLOCKWORK MOUSE, TIMOTHY the wooden lion, and the SKIN HORSE.

(SOUND: clock ticking, wind brushing the windowpanes)

The mechanical toys stand rigidly polished. The old Skin Horse has a bald brown coat, exposed seams, and a thin, tangled tail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Rabbit was naturally shy, and being only made of velveteen, some of the more expensive toys quite snubbed him.

The Rabbit edges close to the Skin Horse.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is Real? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

(SOUND: a coal shifts softly in the grate)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off.

SKIN HORSE
Your eyes drop out, and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse smiles through his worn muzzle.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. Once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

INT. NURSERY - BEDTIME

NANA, a brisk woman in a dark dress and white apron, searches the cluttered floor while the Boy waits in bed.

(SOUND: cupboard door banging, floorboards creaking)

NANA spots the Rabbit in the open cupboard, grabs him gently but quickly by one ear, and places him in the Boy's arms.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

The Boy wraps both arms around the Rabbit beneath the quilt.

(SOUND: soft night wind, the steady tick of a mantel clock)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

The Boy makes a tunnel under the bedclothes and slides the Rabbit through it.

BOY
This is like the burrows the real rabbits live in.

The Boy falls asleep with the Rabbit under his warm chin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the Boy dropped off to sleep, the Rabbit would snuggle down close under his little warm chin.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DAY

The Boy pushes the Rabbit in a wooden wheelbarrow past flower beds and raspberry canes. The Rabbit's pink nose is rubbed pale, his tail hangs loose, and his fur is thinning.

(SOUND: bees buzzing, wheelbarrow wheels rattling over stones)

The Boy builds a tiny fairy hut around the Rabbit beneath the raspberry canes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Wherever the Boy went, the Rabbit went too. He had picnics on the grass and lovely fairy huts built for him behind the flower border.

EXT. GARDEN - DUSK

The Rabbit lies damp and earthy on the lawn, half-hidden beside a flower bed. NANA approaches with a candle lantern.

(SOUND: crickets chirring, wet grass brushing Nana's skirt)

Nana rubs dew from the Rabbit with the corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The Boy sits upright in bed, reaching for the shabby Rabbit. Nana holds him near the night-light.

(SOUND: rain tapping the window, candle flame sputtering)

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Nana places the Rabbit in the Boy's hands. The Rabbit's dull boot-button eyes catch the light.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that, he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him.

INT. NURSERY - MORNING

Nana lifts the Rabbit from the bed. His fur is shabby, his brown spots faded, and his nose is nearly bare, but he rests securely in the Boy's sleeping arms.

(SOUND: birds calling beyond the open window)

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD BEHIND THE HOUSE - JUNE EVENING

The Boy makes a nest of bracken for the Rabbit, then runs away among the trees to play brigands. Ants travel between the Rabbit's velvet paws.

(SOUND: leaves whispering, distant child footsteps, ants rustling through dry fern)

Two WILD RABBITS, soft-furred and quick, creep from the bracken. Their noses twitch as they inspect the motionless toy.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

One Wild Rabbit leaps sideways and rises on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit sits very still, his one-piece velveteen back hidden in the bracken.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

(SOUND: wild rabbits thumping and rustling through ferns)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

The Wild Rabbit dances in a circle.

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still.

The Wild Rabbit's whiskers brush the Rabbit's ear. He recoils.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! The Boy said so!

The Boy's footsteps approach. The wild rabbits flash away into the bracken.

(SOUND: quick feet stamp the earth, bracken snaps, then falls still)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

The Rabbit remains alone as white moths flutter above him.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Weeks later, the Rabbit lies beside the Boy in bed. His whiskers are gone, his ears are grey, his body has lost its shape, and his faded brown-and-white coat is threadbare.

(SOUND: soft breathing, a clock ticking steadily)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy loved him so hard that he loved all his whiskers off.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
To the Boy he was always beautiful, and that was all that the little Rabbit cared about.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The Boy burns with scarlet fever beneath white bedclothes. The Rabbit is clutched against his flushed cheek while unseen adults pass in and out.

(SOUND: strained breathing, basin water sloshing, a low murmur beyond the door)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Strange people came and went in the nursery, and a light burned all night.

The Rabbit stays hidden beneath the covers as the Boy sleeps fitfully.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never stirred, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away.

The Boy's fevered hand tightens around the Rabbit.

(SOUND: the Boy whispers in sleep, rain taps faster against the glass)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And he knew that the Boy needed him.

INT. NURSERY - SUNNY MORNING

The recovered Boy sits wrapped in a shawl on the balcony beside wide-open windows. The Rabbit peeps from tangled bedclothes. NANA and the DOCTOR stand near the bed.

(SOUND: spring birdsong, curtains lifting in the sea-bound breeze)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently the fever turned, and the Boy got better.

The Boy turns a picture-book page with one hand and keeps the Rabbit tucked against his side with the other.

DOCTOR
The room is to be disinfected. All the books and toys he played with in bed must be burnt.

Nana notices the Rabbit.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once.

DOCTOR
Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit stares toward the bright balcony, as if he can already see the sea.

(SOUND: a suitcase latch clicks shut in the next room)

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The Rabbit lies in an untied sack among old picture-books and rubbish. The raspberry canes stand dark beyond him. His thin, worn velveteen offers no warmth.

(SOUND: night wind in the canes, distant fowl shifting in their coop)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night the Boy slept in a different bedroom, and he had a new bunny to sleep with him.

The Rabbit wriggles his head through the sack opening and looks toward the shadowed garden.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear travels down his shabby velvet nose and falls into the soil.

(SOUND: one tear striking dry earth, a far-off owl calling)

A flower pushes from the ground where the tear fell. Emerald leaves unfold around a golden cup of blossom.

(SOUND: a faint magical chime rises beneath the wind)

The blossom opens. The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY steps out, dressed in pearl and dew-drops, flowers wound through her shining hair.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
No.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

The Fairy gathers him carefully into her arms and kisses his damp nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

EXT. WOODLAND GLADE - MOONLIT NIGHT

The Fairy carries the Rabbit through silver bracken into an open glade. Wild rabbits dance with their shadows on velvet grass.

(SOUND: soft paws on turf, night insects humming, branches stirring overhead)

The wild rabbits stop and form a quiet ring around the Fairy.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
He is going to live with you for ever and ever!

The Fairy sets the Rabbit down and kisses him once more.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit remains still, ashamed of his one-piece body. Something tickles his nose; he raises a hind toe to scratch it.

(SOUND: a tiny sneeze, then a bright peal of magical chimes)

His shabby velveteen becomes soft, shining brown fur. Long whiskers brush the grass. His ears twitch, and strong hind legs spring beneath him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He found that he actually had hind legs!

The Rabbit leaps, whirls, and races among the wild rabbits.

(SOUND: rabbits bounding in grass, delighted thumps, music swelling like a gentle waltz)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD BEHIND THE HOUSE - SPRING DAY

A year later, the Boy, older and healthy in a brown coat and cap, plays among the bracken. Two rabbits creep out to watch him.

(SOUND: warm birdsong, leaves rustling, the Boy's stick tapping tree roots)

One rabbit is brown all over. The other is brown with faint old spotted markings beneath his fur and a familiar softness around his round black eyes.

The Boy kneels, watching the marked rabbit.

BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Real Rabbit holds still for one tender moment, then turns toward the bright green wood.

(SOUND: a gentle rabbit thump, breeze moving through the bracken)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The Rabbit bounds after his companions into sunlight.

FADE OUT.

THE END
